        "topic_deep_dive": "The learning rate is a hyperparameter that controls how large a step the optimizer takes in the direction of the negative gradient at each iteration. If the learning rate is too high, the optimizer may overshoot the minimum and fail to converge. If it is too low, convergence becomes very slow, requiring many more iterations to reach the minimum. Choosing an appropriate learning rate is therefore critical to effective optimization. Many advanced optimizers like Adam and RMSProp adapt the learning rate automatically during training to address this challenge.",
        "rubric": []
      },
      {
        "id": "q2",
        "type": "mcq-single",
        "prompt": "What is the primary risk of using a very high learning rate in gradient descent?",
        "sub_concept": "Learning Rate",
        "max_points": 1,
        "options": [
          {
            "id": "q2a",
            "text": "Overshooting the minimum",
