        "topic_deep_dive": "The learning rate governs the step size taken during each parameter update. A high learning rate means the optimizer takes large steps, which can cause it to jump past the minimum of the loss function — a phenomenon known as overshooting. This can lead to oscillation around the minimum or even divergence, where the loss increases rather than decreases. Conversely, a low learning rate results in very small steps, making convergence extremely slow. Adaptive optimizers such as Adam and RMSProp were developed in part to mitigate the sensitivity to learning rate selection.",
        "rubric": []
      },
