        "topic_deep_dive": "Convergence in gradient descent refers to the point at which the optimization algorithm has effectively found a minimum of the loss function and further updates produce negligible change. The teaching content defines convergence as the state when the gradient magnitude approaches zero, meaning the loss landscape is nearly flat at the current parameter values. At this point, the parameter updates become very small, indicating that the optimizer has settled near a minimum. Understanding convergence is important because it helps practitioners decide when to stop training and how to interpret the behavior of the loss curve.",
